<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Italica</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Italica was a Roman city near modern Santiponce, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Scipio Africanus</a> in 206 BCE for wounded veterans of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a>. It was the birthplace of the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> and probably of Hadrian, who enlarged it with a new quarter, and its amphitheatre was one of the largest in the Roman world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/italica.jpg" alt="Italica" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ColdEel · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Italica_schema.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Italica was an ancient Roman city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>, close to the town of Santiponce in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>. It stood near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalquivir">Guadalquivir</a>, close enough to control the area, and near the larger native and Roman city of Hispalis, modern Seville, which would remain the greater of the two. The original old city, the vetus urbs, lies under present-day Santiponce, while the archaeological site covers mainly the new city that Hadrian added to the north.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Italica was the first Roman settlement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. It was founded in 206 BCE by Publius Cornelius Scipio during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a>, close to a native Iberian town of the Turdetani, as a settlement for his Italic veterans, a mixture of allies and Roman citizens, and was named after them. It grew by drawing migrants from the Italian peninsula and the children of Roman soldiers and native women. Among the settlers were a branch of the gens Ulpia from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/todi">Tuder</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umbria">Umbria</a> and a branch of the gens Aelia from Hadria, the families of the emperors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> and Hadrian. According to some authors it was also the birthplace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a>.</p><p>The old city was laid out on a Hippodamian street plan with a forum at the centre, linked to a busy river port. Italica thrived under the patronage of Hadrian, who expanded it northwards as the new city and, at its request, raised it to the status of colonia, although it already held the rights of municipium. He added temples, including the enormous Traianeum, which honoured his predecessor and adoptive father, and rebuilt public buildings. The city began to dwindle as early as the 3rd century, when a shift in the bed of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalquivir">Guadalquivir</a> left its port high and dry. In late antiquity it had a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> and, in the Visigothic age, a garrison, and its walls were restored by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liuvigild">Leovigild</a> in 583.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Because no modern city covers much of the new city, Italica is an unusually well preserved Roman site, with cobbled streets and mosaic floors still in place. Its amphitheatre, the third largest in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> at the time, seated 25,000 spectators in a city whose population is estimated at only 8,000. The Traianeum measured 108 by 80 metres and was decorated with over a hundred columns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cipollino-marble">Cipollino marble</a>, and an aqueduct of 37 kilometres fed a huge cistern that remains intact. Houses of the elite quarter keep splendid mosaics, among them the House of the Birds and the House of the Neptune Mosaic.</p><p>The ruins were long used as a quarry: in 1740 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> ordered the amphitheatre's walls demolished to build a dam. A first law of protection took effect in 1810, and Italica was declared a National Monument by Royal Order of 1912.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Italica” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The emperor controlled the top of the ladder, granting citizenship, equestrian rank and senatorial status, and he renewed the Senate with men from Italy and the provinces: the emperor Trajan himself came from a family settled at <b>Italica</b> in Spain.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancient-rome">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Rome</a></li><li><blockquote>Its elite rose high: Seneca and Lucan came from Corduba, and Trajan and Hadrian from <b>Italica</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/hispania-baetica">Hispania Baetica</a></li></ul></section>
